1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Harald_von_Loudon
2 CONT * http://personen.digitale-sammlungen.de/baltlex/Blatt_bsb00000559,00218.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Harald Georg Gideon von Loudon  was a Baltic German ornithologist.
2 CONT
2 CONT Harald von Loudon was born in the Governorate of Livonia and studied zoology in Saint Petersburg at the Zoological Museum of the Russian Academy of Sciences. He began his ornithological research in the 1890s, making observations n
2 CONC ear his home in the vicinity of Valmiera and Cēsis in present-day Latvia. At this time he started his personal collection of bird eggs. At least from 1894, this collection grew in both size and scope, encompassing not only eggs bu
2 CONC t also whole birds, skeletons and skulls. His first publication appeared in 1895 in the journal of the Riga Association of Natural Scientists (Der Naturforscherverein zu Riga), predecessor of the Natural History Museum of Latvia. 
2 CONC He kept making systematic research of the bird-life of Latvia until at least 1917. In the spring of 1896, he went on a scientific journey to the southern part of the Russian Empire, during which he made the acquaintance of Gustav 
2 CONC Radde and Nikolai Zarudny. The latter became a friend of von Loudon and they kept regular contact. von Loudon came to make an additional five such ornithological expeditions, to the Caucasus, the Caspian Sea, Crimea and Central As
2 CONC ia. In 1904, he hosted Henry Eeles Dresser at his estate in Latvia, and one year later he himself travelled to London to participate in an international conference on ornithology,; he was one of only five Russian citizens to parti
2 CONC cipate.
2 CONT
2 CONT von Loudon accumulated a vast collection of birds, bird eggs and other scientific material, as well as a vast ornithological library. During World War I, von Loudon was forced to flee the country and settled in Germany. His collec
2 CONC tion suffered from mistreatment during the war, but parts of it were rescued and eventually ended up in the University of Latvia; the main part of the collection was however evacuated to present-day Kaliningrad in 1939 and probabl
2 CONC y destroyed there during World War II. The ornithological library ended up in the National Library of Latvia.
